192	<div class="container">
193	
194	<header>
195	  <div class="lesson-id">Lesson 0002 &nbsp;/&nbsp; HFT Trading Practice Repo</div>
196	  <h1>Crossing the Python/C++ Boundary<br>for Numeric Aggregation over Time Series</h1>
197	  <p class="subtitle">How <code>volume_metrics.cpp</code>, <code>bindings.cpp</code>, and <code>fetch_eia()</code> work together — from EIA API to C++ vector to Python list — and all the boundary decisions in between.</p>
198	</header>
199	
200	<div class="toc">
201	  <h4>Contents</h4>
202	  <ol>
203	    <li><a href="#when-cpp">When to move computation to C++</a></li>
204	    <li><a href="#type-system">pybind11 type system: <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> &harr; Python list</a></li>
205	    <li><a href="#nan-handling">NaN handling across the boundary</a></li>
206	    <li><a href="#parity-test">The parity test pattern</a></li>
207	    <li><a href="#eia-api">EIA v2 API: paginated JSON and the cache&rarr;charge&rarr;save flow</a></li>
208	    <li><a href="#api-budget">The APIBudget pattern</a></li>
209	    <li><a href="#non-positive">Non-positive value filtering</a></li>
210	    <li><a href="#return-conventions">Return type conventions: same-length output, NaN for invalid positions</a></li>
211	  </ol>
212	</div>
213	
214	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
215	<h2 id="when-cpp">1. When to Move Computation to C++</h2>
216	
217	<p>Python is expressive and fast to iterate on, but three concrete bottlenecks push inner-loop numeric work into C++:</p>
218	
219	<ul>
220	  <li><strong>Tight loops over time series.</strong> Python executes each iteration as bytecode dispatch with reference-count bookkeeping. For a 10 000-week EIA storage history, a loop over <code>list[float]</code> is 20&ndash;50&times; slower than the equivalent compiled loop over <code>double*</code>.</li>
221	  <li><strong>Cache locality.</strong> A C++ <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> is a contiguous slab of 8-byte values. Python <code>list</code> is an array of pointers to heap-allocated <code>PyFloatObject</code>s (56 bytes each, scattered in memory). The C++ layout fits more elements in an L1 cache line and avoids pointer chasing.</li>
222	  <li><strong>No GIL during compute.</strong> A C extension can release the Global Interpreter Lock (<code>py::gil_scoped_release</code>) and run on a background thread without serialising through the interpreter. Pure Python code cannot do this.</li>
223	</ul>
224	
225	<h3>The Python equivalent vs the C++ loop</h3>
226	
227	<p>The Python reference implementation for <code>capacity_utilization</code> is a straightforward list comprehension:</p>
228	
229	<div class="file-label">Python reference (tests/test_oil_volume.py)</div>
230	<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_py_capacity_utilization</span>(storage_kb, capacity_kb):
231	    <span class="kw">import</span> math
232	    <span class="kw">return</span> [
233	        math.nan <span class="kw">if</span> math.isnan(s) <span class="kw">else</span> s / capacity_kb * <span class="num">100.0</span>
234	        <span class="kw">for</span> s <span class="kw">in</span> storage_kb
235	    ]</pre>
236	
237	<p>The C++ version does exactly the same computation, but iterates over a contiguous buffer with no Python object overhead:</p>
238	
239	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</div>
240	<pre>std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">capacity_utilization</span>(
241	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb, <span class="ty">double</span> capacity_kb)
242	{
243	    <span class="kw">if</span> (capacity_kb &lt;= <span class="num">0.0</span>)
244	        <span class="kw">throw</span> std::invalid_argument(<span class="str">"capacity_kb must be positive"</span>);
245	    std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; out(storage_kb.size());
246	    <span class="kw">for</span> (std::size_t i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; storage_kb.size(); ++i) {
247	        out[i] = std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(storage_kb[i])
248	            ? NaN
249	            : (storage_kb[i] / capacity_kb * <span class="num">100.0</span>);
250	    }
251	    <span class="kw">return</span> out;
252	}</pre>
253	
254	<p>The compiler auto-vectorises this loop on <code>-O3 -march=native</code>, processing four <code>double</code>s per cycle with AVX2 instructions. The Python version cannot benefit from SIMD.</p>
255	
256	<div class="callout info">
257	  <strong>Rule of thumb</strong>
258	  Move to C++ when: (1) the loop runs over more than ~1 000 elements, (2) the per-element operation is arithmetic rather than IO, and (3) the function will be called on every bar or tick. The EIA storage metrics meet all three criteria.
259	</div>
260	
261	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
262	<h2 id="type-system">2. pybind11 Type System: <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> &harr; Python list</h2>
263	
264	<p>When Python calls <code>oil_volume_cpp.capacity_utilization([30000.0, 45000.0], 76000.0)</code>, pybind11's type caster system converts types at the boundary before the C++ function ever sees them.</p>
265	
266	<h3>The critical include</h3>
267	
268	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/bindings.cpp</div>
269	<pre><span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;pybind11/pybind11.h&gt;</span>
270	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</span>    <span class="cmt">// enables std::vector&lt;double&gt; &harr; list[float]</span>
271	
272	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">"volume_metrics.hpp"</span>
273	
274	<span class="kw">namespace</span> py = pybind11;
275	<span class="kw">using namespace</span> oil_volume;
276	
277	<span class="fn">PYBIND11_MODULE</span>(oil_volume_cpp, m) {
278	    m.doc() = <span class="str">"C++ core for oil-volume metrics (pybind11 bridge)"</span>;
279	
280	    m.<span class="fn">def</span>(<span class="str">"capacity_utilization"</span>, &amp;capacity_utilization,
281	        py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"storage_kb"</span>), py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"capacity_kb"</span>), <span class="str">"..."</span>);
282	
283	    m.<span class="fn">def</span>(<span class="str">"days_of_supply"</span>, &amp;days_of_supply,
284	        py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"storage_kb"</span>), py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"production_kbd"</span>), <span class="str">"..."</span>);
285	
286	    m.<span class="fn">def</span>(<span class="str">"fill_rate"</span>, &amp;fill_rate,
287	        py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"storage_kb"</span>), <span class="str">"..."</span>);
288	}</pre>
289	
290	<div class="callout danger">
291	  <strong>Missing &lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt; is a compile error</strong>
292	  Without <code>#include &lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code>, the compiler cannot find a type caster for <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> and will produce a cryptic template error. It does not fall back silently.
293	</div>
294	
295	<h3>What py::arg does</h3>
296	
297	<p><code>py::arg("storage_kb")</code> attaches a keyword-argument name to the C++ parameter. Without it, Python callers must use positional arguments only. With it, both of these are valid:</p>
298	
299	<pre><span class="cmt"># positional</span>
300	cpp.capacity_utilization([<span class="num">30000.0</span>], <span class="num">76000.0</span>)
301	
302	<span class="cmt"># keyword</span>
303	cpp.capacity_utilization(storage_kb=[<span class="num">30000.0</span>], capacity_kb=<span class="num">76000.0</span>)</pre>
304	
305	<h3>The conversion pipeline</h3>
306	
307	<table>
308	  <tr><th>Direction</th><th>Python type</th><th>C++ type</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
309	  <tr><td>Call (in)</td><td><code>list[float]</code></td><td><code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code></td><td>One allocation + element copy</td></tr>
310	  <tr><td>Return (out)</td><td><code>list[float]</code></td><td><code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code></td><td>Move out of C++, then copy to Python list</td></tr>
311	  <tr><td>Scalar (in)</td><td><code>float</code> or <code>int</code></td><td><code>double</code></td><td>Zero allocation</td></tr>
312	  <tr><td>Error</td><td><code>ValueError</code></td><td><code>std::invalid_argument</code></td><td>pybind11 re-raises automatically</td></tr>
313	</table>
314	
315	<p>The conversion is not zero-copy: pybind11 allocates a new <code>std::vector</code> on the way in and a new Python list on the way out. For HFT hot paths, pass NumPy arrays directly (via <code>#include &lt;pybind11/numpy.h&gt;</code>) to work on shared memory. For weekly EIA data (52 elements/year), the copy cost is negligible.</p>
316	
317	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
318	<h2 id="nan-handling">3. NaN Handling Across the Boundary</h2>
319	
320	<p>IEEE 754 NaN ("Not a Number") propagates through both languages, but the API for producing and testing it differs between C++ and Python.</p>
321	
322	<h3>Producing NaN in C++</h3>
323	
324	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</div>
325	<pre><span class="kw">static constexpr</span> <span class="ty">double</span> NaN = std::numeric_limits&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;::quiet_NaN();</pre>
326	
327	<p><code>quiet_NaN()</code> produces a "quiet" NaN — one that propagates through arithmetic without raising a floating-point exception. (A "signalling" NaN raises <code>SIGFPE</code> on first use; never use it for sentinel values.)</p>
328	
329	<h3>Testing NaN in C++ and Python</h3>
330	
331	<table>
332	  <tr><th>Language</th><th>Test</th><th>Why not <code>x == NaN</code></th></tr>
333	  <tr><td>C++</td><td><code>std::isnan(x)</code></td><td>IEEE 754: <code>NaN != NaN</code> is always true</td></tr>
334	  <tr><td>Python</td><td><code>math.isnan(x)</code></td><td>Same reason; <code>float('nan') == float('nan')</code> is <code>False</code></td></tr>
335	</table>
336	
337	<h3>NaN propagation in days_of_supply</h3>
338	
339	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</div>
340	<pre>std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">days_of_supply</span>(
341	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb,
342	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; production_kbd)
343	{
344	    <span class="cmt">// length guard — mismatch is a programmer error, not a data issue</span>
345	    <span class="kw">if</span> (storage_kb.size() != production_kbd.size())
346	        <span class="kw">throw</span> std::invalid_argument(
347	            <span class="str">"storage_kb and production_kbd must be the same length"</span>);
348	
349	    std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; out(storage_kb.size());
350	    <span class="kw">for</span> (std::size_t i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; storage_kb.size(); ++i) {
351	        <span class="kw">if</span> (std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(storage_kb[i])
352	         || std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(production_kbd[i])
353	         || production_kbd[i] &lt;= <span class="num">0.0</span>) {
354	            out[i] = NaN;           <span class="cmt">// explicit propagation</span>
355	        } <span class="kw">else</span> {
356	            out[i] = storage_kb[i] / production_kbd[i];
357	        }
358	    }
359	    <span class="kw">return</span> out;
360	}</pre>
361	
362	<p>Three conditions all produce NaN output: either input is NaN, or production is non-positive. This is the correct defensive pattern: arithmetic on NaN produces NaN anyway, but the non-positive guard prevents a division-by-zero or a semantically nonsensical negative days-of-supply value.</p>
363	
364	<h3>NaN propagation diagram</h3>
365	
366	<div class="diagram"><span class="hi">Input (Python list[float])</span>
367	  [30000.0,  NaN,   60000.0,  0.0  ]   ← storage_kb
368	  [100.0,    200.0,  NaN,     300.0]   ← production_kbd (0.0 treated as invalid)
369	       │
370	       │  pybind11 copies to std::vector&lt;double&gt;
371	       ▼
372	<span class="hi">C++ loop (days_of_supply)</span>
373	  i=0: 30000/100 = 300.0           ← valid
374	  i=1: isnan(storage[1]) → <span class="warn">NaN</span>     ← NaN in, NaN out
375	  i=2: isnan(production[2]) → <span class="warn">NaN</span>  ← NaN in, NaN out
376	  i=3: production &lt;= 0 → <span class="warn">NaN</span>       ← non-positive guard
377	       │
378	       │  pybind11 copies to Python list[float]
379	       ▼
380	<span class="lo">Output (Python list[float])</span>
381	  [300.0, NaN, NaN, NaN]
382	
383	math.isnan(result[1])  →  True     ← Python correctly sees NaN</div>
384	
385	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
386	<h2 id="parity-test">4. The Parity Test Pattern</h2>
387	
388	<p>A parity test compares a C++ result against an independent Python reference implementation and asserts they agree within floating-point tolerance. It serves as both a correctness gate and a regression guard.</p>
389	
390	<h3>The _close() helper</h3>
391	
392	<div class="file-label">tests/test_oil_volume.py</div>
393	<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_close</span>(a, b, tol=<span class="num">1e-9</span>):
394	    <span class="kw">if</span> math.<span class="fn">isnan</span>(a) <span class="kw">and</span> math.<span class="fn">isnan</span>(b):
395	        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">True</span>          <span class="cmt"># NaN == NaN in this context</span>
396	    <span class="kw">if</span> math.<span class="fn">isnan</span>(a) <span class="kw">or</span> math.<span class="fn">isnan</span>(b):
397	        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">False</span>         <span class="cmt"># one NaN, one real number — mismatch</span>
398	    <span class="kw">return</span> abs(a - b) &lt; tol</pre>
399	
400	<p>The helper has three cases: both NaN (equal), one NaN (unequal), or both finite (within tolerance). This handles the IEEE 754 problem where <code>NaN != NaN</code> by convention — in a parity test, if both sides agree on "no data here", that is a successful match.</p>
401	
402	<h3>A complete parity test</h3>
403	
404	<div class="file-label">tests/test_oil_volume.py</div>
405	<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_capacity_utilization_parity</span>(cpp):
406	    storage = [<span class="num">30000.0</span>, <span class="num">45000.0</span>, <span class="num">60000.0</span>, <span class="num">75000.0</span>]
407	    cap = <span class="num">76000.0</span>
408	
409	    got      = cpp.<span class="fn">capacity_utilization</span>(storage, cap)
410	    expected = <span class="fn">_py_capacity_utilization</span>(storage, cap)
411	
412	    <span class="kw">assert</span> all(<span class="fn">_close</span>(g, e) <span class="kw">for</span> g, e <span class="kw">in</span> zip(got, expected))</pre>
413	
414	<h3>Parity test structure</h3>
415	
416	<div class="diagram">                  <span class="hi">same input</span>
417	                      │
418	          ┌───────────┴───────────┐
419	          │                       │
420	          ▼                       ▼
421	<span class="hi">C++ path</span>                    <span class="lo">Python reference</span>
422	cpp.capacity_utilization()   _py_capacity_utilization()
423	          │                       │
424	          │  pybind11 boundary    │  pure Python list comp
425	          ▼                       ▼
426	   list[float]              list[float]
427	          │                       │
428	          └───────────┬───────────┘
429	                      ▼
430	             <span class="fn">_close(g, e, tol=1e-9)</span>
431	             for every element pair
432	                      │
433	             ┌────────┴────────┐
434	             │                 │
435	           PASS              FAIL
436	        both match        logic diverged
437	        (or both NaN)     or NaN mismatch</div>
438	
439	<div class="callout warn">
440	  <strong>Tolerance choice</strong>
441	  <code>1e-9</code> is tight enough to catch logic errors but loose enough to tolerate different floating-point evaluation orders between C++ (which may use FMA instructions) and Python. For integer-valued inputs the result is exact and a tighter tolerance like <code>1e-12</code> is appropriate.
442	</div>
443	
444	<div class="callout good">
445	  <strong>The fixture pattern</strong>
446	  The <code>cpp</code> fixture calls <code>pytest.skip()</code> rather than <code>pytest.fail()</code> when the extension is not built. A missing extension is a configuration problem, not a code bug — so the rest of the test suite keeps running.
447	</div>
448	
449	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
450	<h2 id="eia-api">5. EIA v2 API: Paginated JSON and the Cache&rarr;Charge&rarr;Save Flow</h2>
451	
452	<p>The U.S. Energy Information Administration's v2 API returns results in pages of up to 5 000 rows. A single EIA crude storage series (weekly since 1982) has more than 2 000 rows, so the client must loop until the page returns fewer rows than the page size.</p>
453	
454	<h3>Series ID format</h3>
455	
456	<p>EIA series IDs encode product, geography, and frequency. <code>PET.WCSSTUS1.W</code> decodes as:</p>
457	
458	<table>
459	  <tr><th>Segment</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
460	  <tr><td><code>PET</code></td><td>Petroleum category</td></tr>
461	  <tr><td><code>WCSSTUS1</code></td><td>Weekly Cushing OK crude stock, US</td></tr>
462	  <tr><td><code>W</code></td><td>Weekly frequency</td></tr>
463	</table>
464	
465	<h3>The pagination loop</h3>
466	
467	<div class="file-label">src/oil_alpha/sources.py — fetch_eia() inner _fetch()</div>
468	<pre>rows = []
469	offset = <span class="num">0</span>
470	page_size = <span class="num">5000</span>
471	<span class="kw">while</span> <span class="kw">True</span>:
472	    params = urllib.parse.<span class="fn">urlencode</span>({
473	        <span class="str">"api_key"</span>: key, <span class="str">"start"</span>: start, <span class="str">"end"</span>: end,
474	        <span class="str">"sort[0][column]"</span>: <span class="str">"period"</span>, <span class="str">"sort[0][direction]"</span>: <span class="str">"asc"</span>,
475	        <span class="str">"offset"</span>: offset, <span class="str">"length"</span>: page_size,
476	    })
477	    url = <span class="str">f"https://api.eia.gov/v2/seriesid/{series_id}?{params}"</span>
478	    <span class="kw">with</span> urllib.request.<span class="fn">urlopen</span>(url, timeout=<span class="num">30</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> resp:
479	        payload = json.<span class="fn">loads</span>(resp.<span class="fn">read</span>().<span class="fn">decode</span>(<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>))
480	    data = payload.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="str">"response"</span>, {}).<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="str">"data"</span>, [])
481	    <span class="kw">if not</span> data:
482	        <span class="kw">break</span>
483	    rows.<span class="fn">extend</span>(data)
484	    <span class="kw">if</span> len(data) &lt; page_size:  <span class="cmt"># last page — fewer rows than page_size</span>
485	        <span class="kw">break</span>
486	    offset += page_size</pre>
487	
488	<h3>The data flow end to end</h3>
489	
490	<div class="diagram"><span class="hi">EIA API</span>  https://api.eia.gov/v2/seriesid/PET.WCSSTUS1.W?...
491	    │
492	    │  HTTP GET, possibly multiple pages (offset += 5000)
493	    ▼
494	<span class="hi">Paginated JSON</span>  {"response": {"data": [{period, value, ...}, ...]}}
495	    │
496	    │  json.loads() + pd.DataFrame(rows)
497	    ▼
498	<span class="hi">DataFrame</span>  columns: period (str), value (float), unit, ...
499	    │
500	    │  df["value"] &gt; 0 filter, pd.to_numeric coerce
501	    ▼
502	<span class="hi">Cleaned DataFrame</span>  saved to disk cache (parquet or csv)
503	    │
504	    │  df["value"].tolist()
505	    ▼
506	<span class="lo">list[float]</span>  Python list, one element per week
507	    │
508	    │  pybind11 boundary — cpp.fill_rate(storage_kb)
509	    ▼
510	<span class="lo">std::vector&lt;double&gt;</span>  contiguous 8-byte array in C++ heap
511	    │
512	    │  volume_metrics loop
513	    ▼
514	<span class="lo">std::vector&lt;double&gt;</span>  result
515	    │
516	    │  pybind11 boundary — copy to Python
517	    ▼
518	<span class="hi">list[float]</span>  same length as input, NaN where invalid</div>
519	
520	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
521	<h2 id="api-budget">6. The APIBudget Pattern</h2>
522	
523	<p>An <code>APIBudget</code> tracks network calls by source so that tests and production code can assert that caching is working — a cache hit should never increment the usage counter.</p>
524	
525	<h3>Charge only on network hit</h3>
526	
527	<div class="file-label">src/oil_alpha/sources.py — _from_cache_or()</div>
528	<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_from_cache_or</span>(kind, symbol, start, end, fetch, *, budget, source, use_cache):
529	    <span class="kw">if</span> use_cache:
530	        cached = cache.<span class="fn">load</span>(kind, symbol, start, end)
531	        <span class="kw">if</span> cached <span class="kw">is not None and not</span> cached.empty:
532	            <span class="kw">return</span> cached          <span class="cmt"># cache hit: no charge, return immediately</span>
533	
534	    df = <span class="fn">fetch</span>()                   <span class="cmt"># network hit: calls the HTTP round-trip</span>
535	    budget.<span class="fn">charge</span>(source)          <span class="cmt"># charged ONLY after successful network fetch</span>
536	    <span class="kw">if</span> use_cache <span class="kw">and not</span> df.empty:
537	        cache.<span class="fn">save</span>(df, kind, symbol, start, end)
538	    <span class="kw">return</span> df</pre>
539	
540	<p>The cache check happens before any network I/O. If the cache returns a non-empty DataFrame, the function returns immediately and <code>budget.charge()</code> is never called. This makes the invariant easy to test:</p>
541	
542	<h3>The caching assertion</h3>
543	
544	<div class="file-label">tests/test_oil_volume.py</div>
545	<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_fetch_eia_caches_on_second_call</span>(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
546	    <span class="cmt"># ... setup fake cache with pre-populated data ...</span>
547	    budget = <span class="fn">APIBudget</span>()
548	
549	    result = <span class="fn">fetch_eia</span>(
550	        <span class="str">"PET.WCSSTUS1.W"</span>, <span class="str">"2023-01-01"</span>, <span class="str">"2023-01-31"</span>,
551	        budget=budget, use_cache=<span class="kw">True</span>, api_key=<span class="str">"test-key"</span>
552	    )
553	
554	    eia_usage = budget.usage.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="str">"eia"</span>)
555	    <span class="kw">assert</span> eia_usage <span class="kw">is None or</span> eia_usage.used == <span class="num">0</span>  <span class="cmt"># cache hit = free</span></pre>
556	
557	<h3>Budget charge flow</h3>
558	
559	<div class="diagram">fetch_eia("PET.WCSSTUS1.W", ...)
560	    │
561	    ▼
562	_from_cache_or(...)
563	    │
564	    ├─── cache.load() ──▶ <span class="lo">hit (not None and not empty)</span>
565	    │                         │
566	    │                         └──▶ return cached  ← <span class="lo">budget.charge() SKIPPED</span>
567	    │
568	    └─── cache.load() ──▶ <span class="warn">miss (None or empty)</span>
569	                              │
570	                              ▼
571	                          _fetch() ── HTTP GET ──▶ EIA API
572	                              │
573	                              ▼
574	                          budget.<span class="hi">charge</span>("eia")   ← <span class="hi">counted here</span>
575	                              │
576	                              ▼
577	                          cache.save(df, ...)
578	                              │
579	                              ▼
580	                          return df</div>
