582	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
583	<h2 id="non-positive">7. Non-Positive Value Filtering</h2>
584	
585	<p>Oil storage values must be strictly positive — zero or negative values in the EIA response indicate missing data, reporting errors, or API artefacts. The filter is applied at both the Python ingestion layer and the C++ computation layer, for defence in depth.</p>
586	
587	<h3>Python ingestion filter</h3>
588	
589	<div class="file-label">src/oil_alpha/sources.py — fetch_eia()</div>
590	<pre>df[<span class="str">"value"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[<span class="str">"value"</span>], errors=<span class="str">"coerce"</span>)
591	df = df[df[<span class="str">"value"</span>] &gt; <span class="num">0</span>].<span class="fn">copy</span>()   <span class="cmt"># drop NaN, zero, and negative rows</span></pre>
592	
593	<p><code>pd.to_numeric(..., errors="coerce")</code> first converts the JSON string <code>"value"</code> field to a float, coercing non-numeric strings to <code>NaN</code>. The subsequent <code>> 0</code> filter drops both those NaN rows and any non-positive numerics in a single step.</p>
594	
595	<h3>C++ computation guard</h3>
596	
597	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp — days_of_supply()</div>
598	<pre><span class="kw">if</span> (std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(storage_kb[i])
599	 || std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(production_kbd[i])
600	 || production_kbd[i] &lt;= <span class="num">0.0</span>) {   <span class="cmt">// &lt;= 0 guard here</span>
601	    out[i] = NaN;
602	}</pre>
603	
604	<p>Even if clean data is guaranteed from the Python side, the C++ guard prevents a division-by-zero and ensures the output is always semantically meaningful. A days-of-supply value computed against zero or negative production would be infinite or negative — both are nonsensical for a trading signal.</p>
605	
606	<div class="callout good">
607	  <strong>Why filter in both places?</strong>
608	  The Python filter removes bad rows from storage. The C++ guard handles NaN values that can arise from other computation paths (e.g., a fill_rate output fed into days_of_supply) — not just from the raw EIA data. Each layer catches what the other cannot.
609	</div>
610	
611	<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
612	<h2 id="return-conventions">8. Return Type Conventions: Same-Length Output, NaN for Invalid Positions</h2>
613	
614	<p>All four C++ metric functions share the same output contract: the returned vector is always exactly the same length as the input, and positions that cannot produce a meaningful value are filled with <code>NaN</code> rather than being dropped or signalled with a special integer sentinel.</p>
615	
616	<h3>Why same-length output?</h3>
617	
618	<ul>
619	  <li><strong>Alignment with the time axis.</strong> If the caller has a <code>DataFrame</code> with a <code>period</code> column, they can assign the result directly as a new column without reindexing.</li>
620	  <li><strong>Composability.</strong> Downstream functions can safely zip two outputs from the same input without length-mismatch errors.</li>
621	  <li><strong>Explicit invalidity.</strong> NaN is a first-class float value. A downstream chart or signal engine can test <code>math.isnan(v)</code> to skip invalid positions without needing a parallel boolean mask array.</li>
622	</ul>
623	
624	<h3>fill_rate: the canonical example</h3>
625	
626	<p><code>fill_rate</code> is the week-over-week change in storage. By definition, the first element has no prior period — there is nothing to subtract from. The output is initialised entirely to NaN, then filled from index 1 onward:</p>
627	
628	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</div>
629	<pre>std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">fill_rate</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb) {
630	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::size_t n = storage_kb.size();
631	    std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; out(n, NaN);   <span class="cmt">// ALL elements initialised to NaN</span>
632	
633	    <span class="kw">for</span> (std::size_t i = <span class="num">1</span>; i &lt; n; ++i) {   <span class="cmt">// start at 1, not 0</span>
634	        <span class="kw">if</span> (std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(storage_kb[i]) || std::<span class="fn">isnan</span>(storage_kb[i - <span class="num">1</span>])) {
635	            out[i] = NaN;          <span class="cmt">// NaN input propagates</span>
636	        } <span class="kw">else</span> {
637	            out[i] = storage_kb[i] - storage_kb[i - <span class="num">1</span>];
638	        }
639	    }
640	    <span class="kw">return</span> out;   <span class="cmt">// out[0] is always NaN — never touched by the loop</span>
641	}</pre>
642	
643	<h3>Output layout across all four functions</h3>
644	
645	<table>
646	  <tr><th>Function</th><th>NaN positions</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
647	  <tr><td><code>capacity_utilization</code></td><td>Where <code>storage_kb[i]</code> is NaN</td><td>Invalid input propagated</td></tr>
648	  <tr><td><code>days_of_supply</code></td><td>Where either input is NaN or production &le; 0</td><td>NaN propagation + non-positive guard</td></tr>
649	  <tr><td><code>fill_rate</code></td><td>Index 0 always; index i where either of its two inputs is NaN</td><td>No prior period at index 0</td></tr>
650	  <tr><td><code>rolling_mean</code></td><td>Indices 0 &hellip; window&minus;2</td><td>Window not yet full</td></tr>
651	</table>
652	
653	<div class="diagram"><span class="hi">fill_rate example</span>
654	
655	Input:  [<span class="num">400</span>,  <span class="num">420</span>,   NaN,   <span class="num">410</span>,  <span class="num">430</span>]
656	
657	Init:   [<span class="warn">NaN</span>,  <span class="warn">NaN</span>,   <span class="warn">NaN</span>,   <span class="warn">NaN</span>,  <span class="warn">NaN</span>]  ← std::vector&lt;double&gt; out(n, NaN)
658	
659	Loop i=1: 420-400 = 20    → out[1] = 20
660	Loop i=2: isnan(NaN)      → out[2] = <span class="warn">NaN</span>
661	Loop i=3: isnan(prev NaN) → out[3] = <span class="warn">NaN</span>
662	Loop i=4: 430-410 = 20    → out[4] = 20
663	
664	Output: [<span class="warn">NaN</span>,  <span class="num">20</span>,    <span class="warn">NaN</span>,   <span class="warn">NaN</span>,  <span class="num">20</span>]
665	         ^                  ^      ^
666	         always NaN         propagated NaN (gap in input)</div>
667	
668	<div class="callout info">
669	  <strong>HPP declaration review</strong>
670	  The header <code>volume_metrics.hpp</code> declares all four functions inside the <code>oil_volume</code> namespace. The <code>bindings.cpp</code> file uses <code>using namespace oil_volume</code> so that the <code>m.def</code> calls can reference <code>capacity_utilization</code> without the namespace prefix — matching the Python-facing name exactly.
671	</div>
672	
673	<div class="file-label">cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.hpp</div>
674	<pre><span class="pp">#pragma once</span>
675	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;vector&gt;</span>
676	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;cstddef&gt;</span>
677	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;limits&gt;</span>
678	
679	<span class="kw">namespace</span> oil_volume {
680	std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">capacity_utilization</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb, <span class="ty">double</span> capacity_kb);
681	std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">days_of_supply</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb, <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; production_kbd);
682	std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">fill_rate</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb);
683	std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">rolling_mean</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; values, <span class="ty">int</span> window);
684	}
685	</pre>
