277	<!-- Q2 -->
278	<div class="question-block" id="q2">
279	  <div class="q-num">Question 2</div>
280	  <div class="q-text">Why does <code>bindings.cpp</code> include <code>&lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code> in addition to <code>&lt;pybind11/pybind11.h&gt;</code>?</div>
281	  <div class="options">
282	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>To enable multi-threading support</button>
283	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>To link against the C++ standard library at compile time</button>
284	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>To activate the type casters that convert <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> to a Python <code>list</code> and vice versa</button>
285	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>To include the STL allocator so pybind11 can allocate Python objects from a C++ allocator</button>
286	  </div>
287	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q2-fb-correct">Correct. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> provides type caster specialisations for standard containers: <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::map</code>, <code>std::set</code>, etc. Without it, the compiler would error: "cannot convert argument of type std::vector".</div>
288	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q2-fb-wrong">No. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> is about type casters — it tells pybind11 how to convert C++ standard containers to their Python equivalents (and back) at the call boundary.</div>
289	</div>
290	
291	<!-- Q3 -->
292	<div class="question-block" id="q3">
293	  <div class="q-num">Question 3</div>
294	  <div class="q-text">What problem does <code>set(PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON ON)</code> in CMakeLists.txt solve?</div>
295	  <div class="options">
296	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>It switches from static linking to dynamic linking of the pybind11 library</button>
297	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>It makes pybind11 use the modern <code>FindPython3</code> CMake module instead of the deprecated <code>FindPythonLibs</code>, allowing it to correctly find headers in virtual environments and uv-managed Pythons</button>
298	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>It enables Python to find the compiled extension without modifying <code>sys.path</code></button>
299	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>It disables the default Python discovery so the user must supply <code>-DPython3_EXECUTABLE</code> manually</button>
300	  </div>
301	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q3-fb-correct">Correct. The legacy <code>FindPythonLibs</code> only searches system paths. <code>FindPython3</code> derives header paths from any Python executable you point it at — essential for venv and uv-managed interpreters.</div>
302	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q3-fb-wrong">Not right. The flag tells pybind11 which CMake Python-discovery module to use: the modern <code>FindPython3</code> (which works with venvs and uv Pythons) vs the deprecated <code>FindPythonLibs</code> (which only looks at system paths).</div>
303	</div>
304	
305	<!-- Q4 -->
306	<div class="question-block" id="q4">
307	  <div class="q-num">Question 4</div>
308	  <div class="q-text">What does <code>pybind11_add_module(oil_volume_cpp bindings.cpp)</code> do that a plain <code>add_library(oil_volume_cpp SHARED bindings.cpp)</code> does not?</div>
309	  <div class="options">
310	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>It enables C++17 on the target automatically</button>
311	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>It copies the <code>.so</code> file into <code>site-packages</code> after linking</button>
312	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>It sets the correct Python extension suffix, strips the <code>lib</code> prefix, links Python headers, applies <code>-fvisibility=hidden</code>, and enables LTO in Release mode</button>
313	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>It runs the pybind11 code generator to produce the <code>PyInit_</code> function</button>
314	  </div>
315	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q4-fb-correct">Correct. That single CMake function handles all the Python-specific build details so you don't have to set them manually.</div>
316	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q4-fb-wrong">No. <code>pybind11_add_module</code> is a smart wrapper around <code>add_library(SHARED)</code> that sets the correct Python extension suffix, strips the lib prefix, applies <code>-fvisibility=hidden</code>, and enables LTO in Release mode.</div>
317	</div>
318	
319	<!-- Q5 -->
320	<div class="question-block" id="q5">
321	  <div class="q-num">Question 5</div>
322	  <div class="q-text">In the hybrid <code>find_package</code> / <code>FetchContent</code> strategy in this repo, what happens when <code>find_package(pybind11 CONFIG QUIET)</code> succeeds?</div>
323	  <div class="options">
324	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',0,true)"><span class="option-key">A</span>CMake uses the pip-installed pybind11 CMake config files and skips the <code>FetchContent_Declare</code> block entirely — no network call is made</button>
325	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>CMake still runs <code>FetchContent_MakeAvailable</code> but uses it only for the version check</button>
326	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>CMake downloads pybind11 from GitHub and overwrites the pip-installed one</button>
327	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>CMake prefers the FetchContent version if the network is available</button>
328	  </div>
329	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q5-fb-correct">Correct. The <code>if(NOT pybind11_FOUND)</code> guard means the FetchContent block only runs when <code>find_package</code> fails. This makes configure-time fast when pybind11 is pip-installed.</div>
330	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q5-fb-wrong">The <code>if(NOT pybind11_FOUND)</code> guard in CMakeLists.txt skips the <code>FetchContent_Declare</code> entirely when <code>find_package</code> succeeds. No network call is made.</div>
331	</div>
