382	<!-- R1 -->
383	<div class="recall-block">
384	  <div class="q-num">Recall 1</div>
385	  <div class="q-text">Explain the complete chain of events that happens when Python executes <code>import oil_volume_cpp</code> — from disk I/O through to a callable function being available in the namespace. Include the role of the ABI tag, <code>dlopen</code>, and the <code>PyInit_</code> function.</div>
386	  <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here..."></textarea>
387	  <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="revealAnswer(this)">Show model answer</button>
388	  <div class="model-answer">
389	    <strong>Model answer</strong>
390	    Python's import system searches <code>sys.path</code> for a file matching <code>oil_volume_cpp.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so</code> (or platform equivalent). When found, Python calls <code>dlopen()</code> on the <code>.so</code>, loading the compiled native code into the process's address space. The dynamic linker resolves any shared library dependencies (e.g. <code>libstdc++</code>). Python then looks up the symbol <code>PyInit_oil_volume_cpp</code> — which <code>PYBIND11_MODULE</code> expanded to — and calls it. That function creates a <code>py::module_</code> object, registers all <code>m.def()</code> entries as Python callable objects, and returns the module. Python stores it in <code>sys.modules["oil_volume_cpp"]</code> so subsequent imports are cache hits. The ABI tag ensures Python only loads a <code>.so</code> compiled for its exact interpreter version; a tag mismatch causes an <code>ImportError</code> before <code>dlopen</code> is attempted.
391	  </div>
392	</div>
393	
394	<!-- R2 -->
395	<div class="recall-block">
396	  <div class="q-num">Recall 2</div>
397	  <div class="q-text">Describe the purpose of each of the three key files in the build system: <code>CMakeLists.txt</code> (root), <code>cpp/oil_volume/CMakeLists.txt</code>, and <code>pyproject.toml</code>. What would break if you removed each one?</div>
398	  <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here..."></textarea>
399	  <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="revealAnswer(this)">Show model answer</button>
400	  <div class="model-answer">
401	    <strong>Model answer</strong>
402	    <strong>Root CMakeLists.txt:</strong> The top-level CMake project file. It declares the project, sets C++17, finds or fetches pybind11, sets <code>PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON=ON</code>, and delegates to the subdirectory. Without it CMake has no entry point and cannot be configured at all.<br><br>
403	    <strong>cpp/oil_volume/CMakeLists.txt:</strong> Defines the actual build target via <code>pybind11_add_module(oil_volume_cpp bindings.cpp)</code>. Without it the <code>add_subdirectory</code> call in the root would fail, and no shared library would be produced.<br><br>
404	    <strong>pyproject.toml:</strong> Configures the Python build backend (scikit-build-core). It is the glue between <code>pip install</code> and the CMake build system. Without it, <code>pip install -e .</code> would not know to invoke CMake — pip would try to use setuptools and find no setup.py, failing with an error.
405	  </div>
406	</div>
407	
408	<!-- R3 -->
409	<div class="recall-block">
410	  <div class="q-num">Recall 3</div>
411	  <div class="q-text">You want to add a second function <code>rolling_std(values, window)</code> to the extension. List every file you need to touch, what change to make in each, and the command to rebuild. Assume the C++ implementation is already written.</div>
412	  <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here..."></textarea>
413	  <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="revealAnswer(this)">Show model answer</button>
414	  <div class="model-answer">
415	    <strong>Model answer</strong>
416	    1. <strong><code>cpp/oil_volume/bindings.cpp</code></strong> — Add the C++ implementation of <code>rolling_std</code> (or include its header), then add inside <code>PYBIND11_MODULE(oil_volume_cpp, m)</code>: <code>m.def("rolling_std", &amp;rolling_std, py::arg("values"), py::arg("window"), "docstring");</code><br><br>
417	    2. <strong><code>cpp/oil_volume/CMakeLists.txt</code></strong> — If <code>rolling_std</code> is in a separate <code>.cpp</code> file, add it to <code>pybind11_add_module(oil_volume_cpp bindings.cpp rolling_std.cpp)</code>. If it is all in <code>bindings.cpp</code>, no change needed.<br><br>
418	    3. <strong><code>tests/test_cpp_bridge.py</code></strong> — Add a parity test comparing <code>cpp_module.rolling_std(values, window)</code> against a NumPy reference within <code>1e-9</code>.<br><br>
419	    Rebuild command: <code>pip install -e .</code> (or <code>./scripts/build_cpp.sh</code>), then restart the interpreter. No changes needed to the root CMakeLists.txt or pyproject.toml.
420	  </div>
