272	
273	<!-- Q1 -->
274	<div class="question-block" id="q1">
275	  <div class="q-num">Question 1</div>
276	  <div class="q-text">What does <code>#include &lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code> enable in <code>bindings.cpp</code>?</div>
277	  <div class="options">
278	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>Allows C++ exceptions to propagate to Python</button>
279	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>Enables automatic conversion between <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> and Python <code>list</code></button>
280	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>Provides SIMD intrinsics for fast vector operations</button>
281	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>Enables multi-threading support in pybind11</button>
282	  </div>
283	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q1-fb-correct">Correct. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> registers type caster specialisations for standard containers. Without it, passing a <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> across the boundary would fail to compile — pybind11 would have no way to convert it to a Python <code>list</code> and back.</div>
284	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q1-fb-wrong">Not quite. Exception propagation is handled automatically by pybind11 core (not <code>stl.h</code>); SIMD intrinsics are a separate library; GIL/threading is separate too. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> specifically provides the type casters that convert <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> &harr; Python <code>list</code>.</div>
285	</div>
286	
287	<!-- Q2 -->
288	<div class="question-block" id="q2">
289	  <div class="q-num">Question 2</div>
290	  <div class="q-text">In <code>volume_metrics.cpp</code>, <code>static constexpr double NaN = std::numeric_limits&lt;double&gt;::quiet_NaN()</code>. What property makes this the right NaN to use?</div>
291	  <div class="options">
292	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>It compares equal to itself</button>
293	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>It is a signaling NaN that raises a hardware exception on use</button>
294	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>It is a quiet NaN that propagates through arithmetic without raising hardware exceptions</button>
295	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>It is only defined on x86 platforms</button>
296	  </div>
297	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q2-fb-correct">Correct. A quiet NaN propagates silently through arithmetic operations — any computation involving it produces another NaN, alerting downstream code that the value is unknown without triggering a hardware signal. Signaling NaN (<code>std::numeric_limits&lt;double&gt;::signaling_NaN()</code>) would cause a floating-point exception on first use, which is not what we want for "no data" sentinels.</div>
298	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q2-fb-wrong">NaN never equals itself (IEEE 754 defines NaN != NaN always). A signaling NaN would raise a hardware exception on use — that is <code>signaling_NaN()</code>, the wrong choice here. <code>quiet_NaN()</code> is standard C++11, not x86-specific. The right answer: quiet NaN propagates silently through arithmetic.</div>
299	</div>
300	
301	<!-- Q3 -->
302	<div class="question-block" id="q3">
303	  <div class="q-num">Question 3</div>
304	  <div class="q-text">In the parity test, why can't you use <code>a == b</code> to compare NaN values returned by the C++ extension?</div>
305	  <div class="options">
306	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>Python float comparison raises <code>TypeError</code> for NaN</button>
307	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span><code>NaN != NaN</code> by IEEE 754 definition, so <code>NaN == NaN</code> is always <code>False</code></button>
308	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>C++ and Python use different NaN bit patterns</button>
309	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>The <code>==</code> operator is not available for floats in Python</button>
310	  </div>
311	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q3-fb-correct">Correct. IEEE 754 mandates that any comparison involving NaN — including <code>NaN == NaN</code> — returns <code>False</code>. Python returns <code>False</code> silently rather than raising an exception. The parity test therefore needs a <code>_close()</code> helper that handles NaN with <code>math.isnan(a) and math.isnan(b)</code>.</div>
312	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q3-fb-wrong">Python does not raise <code>TypeError</code> — it returns <code>False</code> silently. Both C++ and Python use the same IEEE 754 bit pattern for quiet NaN. The <code>==</code> operator is available for Python floats. The real reason: IEEE 754 defines <code>NaN != NaN</code>, so <code>NaN == NaN</code> is always <code>False</code>.</div>
313	</div>
314	
315	<!-- Q4 -->
316	<div class="question-block" id="q4">
317	  <div class="q-num">Question 4</div>
318	  <div class="q-text">The EIA v2 series ID <code>PET.WCSSTUS1.W</code> — what does the trailing <code>.W</code> indicate?</div>
319	  <div class="options">
320	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>The data is from the West region</button>
321	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>The data uses WTI price units</button>
322	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>The data is weekly frequency</button>
323	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>The data is Washington DC-specific</button>
324	  </div>
325	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q4-fb-correct">Correct. In EIA v2 series IDs the trailing frequency code <code>.W</code> means Weekly. <code>WCSSTUS1</code> is the series for Cushing, Oklahoma crude oil stocks (US total) — a storage/inventory series, not a price or region code.</div>
326	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q4-fb-wrong">W is the frequency code for Weekly, not West, WTI, or Washington. <code>WCSSTUS1</code> refers to Cushing, Oklahoma crude oil stocks (US total). A monthly series would end in <code>.M</code>, annual in <code>.A</code>.</div>
327	</div>
328	
329	<!-- Q5 -->
330	<div class="question-block" id="q5">
331	  <div class="q-num">Question 5</div>
332	  <div class="q-text"><code>fetch_eia()</code> uses pagination. When does the <code>while</code> loop break?</div>
333	  <div class="options">
334	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>After exactly one request</button>
335	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>When <code>len(data) &lt; page_size</code> (fewer rows than requested) or when <code>data</code> is empty</button>
336	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>After the budget is exhausted</button>
337	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>When the EIA API returns a 404 status</button>
338	  </div>
339	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q5-fb-correct">Correct. If the API returns fewer rows than the requested page size, there are no more pages — the loop breaks. An empty response is also a break condition. The budget charge happens once after all pages are collected, not inside the loop; a 404 would raise an exception rather than break cleanly.</div>
340	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q5-fb-wrong">A single-request break would miss multi-page datasets. Budget is charged once after <code>_fetch()</code> returns, not inside the pagination loop. A 404 raises an exception. The correct termination condition: <code>len(data) &lt; page_size</code> signals the last page has been received.</div>
341	</div>
342	
343	<!-- Q6 -->
344	<div class="question-block" id="q6">
345	  <div class="q-num">Question 6</div>
346	  <div class="q-text">In <code>_from_cache_or()</code>, when does <code>budget.charge(source)</code> get called?</div>
347	  <div class="options">
348	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>Every time the function is called</button>
349	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>Only when <code>use_cache=False</code></button>
350	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>Only after a real network fetch (<code>df = fetch()</code> was called)</button>
351	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>Never — the <code>APIBudget</code> is only for tracking purposes</button>
352	  </div>
353	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q6-fb-correct">Correct. The cache check comes first: if <code>cache.load()</code> returns data, the function returns immediately and <code>budget.charge()</code> is never reached. The charge only executes when <code>fetch()</code> actually ran — i.e., a real network call was made. This makes cache hits literally free in budget terms.</div>
354	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q6-fb-wrong">Cache hits skip the charge entirely — the function returns before reaching <code>budget.charge()</code>. <code>use_cache=False</code> forces a fetch, which would trigger a charge, but the charge criterion is whether <code>fetch()</code> ran, not whether <code>use_cache</code> is False. The budget is not just for tracking — it enforces real API call accounting.</div>
355	</div>
356	
357	<!-- Q7 -->
358	<div class="question-block" id="q7">
359	  <div class="q-num">Question 7</div>
360	  <div class="q-text">Why does <code>fill_rate()</code> initialize <code>out(n, NaN)</code> instead of <code>out(n, 0.0)</code>?</div>
361	  <div class="options">
362	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>NaN is faster than 0.0 for SIMD operations</button>
363	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>The first element has no prior period, so NaN is the semantically correct "unknown" value; any index not written later is also correctly NaN</button>
364	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>pybind11 requires NaN as the default for uninitialized vectors</button>
365	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>0.0 would cause division by zero in downstream code</button>
366	  </div>
367	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q7-fb-correct">Correct. <code>fill_rate</code> computes period-over-period differences. The first element genuinely has no previous period — 0.0 would be a fabricated number that could mislead callers. NaN correctly signals "this position is undefined." Any other index left unwritten (e.g., due to a gap) is also automatically NaN, which is the right sentinel.</div>
368	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q7-fb-wrong">NaN has no SIMD speed advantage. pybind11 has no such requirement. <code>fill_rate</code> returns differences, not ratios, so 0.0 would not cause division by zero. The correct reason: the first element has no prior period, making NaN the semantically appropriate "unknown" value.</div>
369	</div>
370	
371	<!-- Q8 -->
372	<div class="question-block" id="q8">
373	  <div class="q-num">Question 8</div>
374	  <div class="q-text">In <code>days_of_supply()</code>, why is <code>production_kbd[i] &lt;= 0.0</code> mapped to NaN instead of throwing an exception?</div>
375	  <div class="options">
376	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>C++ cannot throw exceptions through the pybind11 boundary</button>
377	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>Non-positive production is a valid data condition (e.g., a reporting gap), so NaN is the appropriate "no data" signal rather than an error</button>
378	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>The EIA API sometimes returns zero as a sentinel value and the caller must decide</button>
379	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>Throwing would invalidate the same-length output invariant</button>
380	  </div>
381	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q8-fb-correct">Correct. Reporting gaps and revisions can produce zero or negative production values in raw data — these are not programming errors, they are data quality conditions. NaN propagates the "no data" signal cleanly through subsequent calculations. Throwing an exception would be inappropriate for a routine data-quality condition.</div>
382	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q8-fb-wrong">pybind11 absolutely can translate C++ exceptions to Python exceptions. Same-length output is maintained by pre-allocating <code>out</code>, not by avoiding throws. While EIA may use zero as a sentinel, the core reason for NaN is that non-positive production is a valid data condition, not a programming error — NaN is the established floating-point "unknown" signal.</div>
383	</div>
384	
385	<!-- Q9 -->
386	<div class="question-block" id="q9">
387	  <div class="q-num">Question 9 (Bonus)</div>
388	  <div class="q-text">Which Python expression correctly detects a NaN received from the C++ extension?</div>
389	  <div class="options">
390	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q9',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span><code>x == float('nan')</code></button>
391	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q9',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span><code>math.isnan(x)</code></button>
392	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q9',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span><code>x is None</code></button>
393	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q9',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span><code>numpy.isnan(x)</code> — this is the only correct method</button>
394	  </div>
395	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q9-fb-correct">Correct. <code>math.isnan(x)</code> is the standard library function for detecting NaN in a plain Python <code>float</code>. Note that <code>numpy.isnan(x)</code> also works — both are correct. The question is calling out that D's claim of "only correct method" is false.</div>
396	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q9-fb-wrong"><code>x == float('nan')</code> always returns <code>False</code> (NaN != NaN by IEEE 754). NaN is a <code>float</code> value, not <code>None</code>, so <code>x is None</code> is always <code>False</code>. <code>numpy.isnan</code> works but is not the <em>only</em> method — <code>math.isnan(x)</code> is the correct standard-library answer for a plain Python float.</div>
397	</div>
