406	<!-- R1 -->
407	<div class="recall-block">
408	  <div class="q-num">Recall 1</div>
409	  <div class="q-text">Describe the data flow from an EIA API response to a C++ vector computation. Name each transformation step.</div>
410	  <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here..."></textarea>
411	  <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="revealAnswer(this)">Show model answer</button>
412	  <div class="model-answer">
413	    <strong>Model answer</strong>
414	    1. <code>fetch_eia()</code> calls <code>cache.load()</code> — if a cache hit, return immediately (no charge, no network).<br><br>
415	    2. On a cache miss: HTTP GET to <code>api.eia.gov/v2/seriesid/{series_id}</code> with pagination parameters (<code>offset</code>, <code>length</code>).<br><br>
416	    3. JSON response parsed &rarr; list of row dicts, each with <code>"period"</code> and <code>"value"</code> keys.<br><br>
417	    4. Pagination loop: repeat until <code>len(data) &lt; page_size</code> or <code>data</code> is empty, appending each page.<br><br>
418	    5. <code>pd.DataFrame(rows)</code> constructed &rarr; <code>df["value"] = pd.to_numeric(..., errors="coerce")</code> converts strings and coerces bad values to NaN.<br><br>
419	    6. Non-positive filter: <code>df[df["value"] &gt; 0]</code> drops zero/negative rows.<br><br>
420	    7. <code>budget.charge("eia")</code> — cost recorded after the real fetch completes.<br><br>
421	    8. <code>cache.save()</code> — the result is stored so future calls within the session are free.<br><br>
422	    9. <code>.value.tolist()</code> (or equivalent) &rarr; Python <code>list[float]</code>.<br><br>
423	    10. pybind11 type caster (activated by <code>#include &lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code>) copies the Python list &rarr; <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> on the C++ side.<br><br>
424	    11. C++ metric function (<code>capacity_utilization</code>, <code>days_of_supply</code>, or <code>fill_rate</code>) runs over the vector and returns a <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code>.<br><br>
425	    12. pybind11 type caster converts the result back &rarr; Python <code>list[float]</code>.
426	  </div>
427	</div>
428	
429	<!-- R2 -->
430	<div class="recall-block">
431	  <div class="q-num">Recall 2</div>
432	  <div class="q-text">Explain the parity test pattern. Why do we need a special <code>_close()</code> function instead of just <code>==</code>?</div>
433	  <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here..."></textarea>
434	  <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="revealAnswer(this)">Show model answer</button>
435	  <div class="model-answer">
436	    <strong>Model answer</strong>
437	    The parity test runs the same inputs through both the C++ implementation and a simple Python reference implementation, then asserts the outputs match element-by-element within a tolerance (typically <code>1e-9</code>).<br><br>
438	    We need <code>_close()</code> instead of <code>==</code> for two distinct reasons:<br><br>
439	    <strong class="inline">1. NaN identity:</strong> <code>NaN != NaN</code> by IEEE 754, so if both the C++ and Python outputs are NaN at the same position, <code>==</code> would return <code>False</code> even though both are correct. <code>_close()</code> handles this with <code>math.isnan(a) and math.isnan(b) &rarr; True</code>.<br><br>
440	    <strong class="inline">2. Floating-point rounding:</strong> Different evaluation orders, compiler optimizations (e.g., fused multiply-add instructions), and intermediate precision can cause C++ and Python to produce results that differ in the last few ULPs (units in the last place) even for mathematically identical operations. A tolerance of <code>1e-9</code> is tight enough to catch real logic bugs but wide enough to ignore benign rounding differences.
441	  </div>
442	</div>
443	
444	<!-- R3 -->
445	<div class="recall-block">
446	  <div class="q-num">Recall 3</div>
447	  <div class="q-text">What is the <code>APIBudget</code> pattern and why is a cache hit free?</div>
448	  <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here..."></textarea>
449	  <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="revealAnswer(this)">Show model answer</button>
450	  <div class="model-answer">
451	    <strong>Model answer</strong>
452	    <code>APIBudget</code> tracks how many real network calls have been made to each external API (e.g., <code>"eia"</code>). The charge is recorded in <code>_from_cache_or()</code> immediately after calling <code>fetch()</code> — not inside <code>fetch()</code> itself and not at the top of the outer function.<br><br>
453	    The execution order in <code>_from_cache_or()</code> is:<br>
454	    1. <code>cache.load()</code> &mdash; if data is returned, <code>return</code> immediately.<br>
455	    2. <code>df = fetch()</code> &mdash; real network call.<br>
456	    3. <code>budget.charge(source)</code> &mdash; cost recorded.<br>
457	    4. <code>cache.save(df)</code> &mdash; result persisted.<br><br>
458	    A cache hit is literally free because the function returns at step 1 — <code>budget.charge()</code> on step 3 is never reached. This design means you can call <code>fetch_eia()</code> many times within a session without accumulating budget charges; only the first call (per unique series/window combination) is billed. Subsequent calls are served from disk cache at zero cost.
459	  </div>
460	</div>
