203	<!-- ═══════════════════════ quiet_NaN ═══════════════════════ -->
204	<div class="term-card" id="quiet-nan">
205	  <div class="term-header">
206	    <span class="term-name">quiet_NaN</span>
207	    <span class="term-type type-macro">C++ / IEEE 754</span>
208	  </div>
209	
210	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
211	  <p class="definition">An IEEE 754 floating-point special value produced by <code>std::numeric_limits&lt;double&gt;::quiet_NaN()</code> that propagates silently through arithmetic without raising hardware exceptions (unlike signaling NaN). Any arithmetic with quiet_NaN yields another NaN, so downstream consumers see the gap rather than a corrupt finite value.</p>
212	
213	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
214	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</span>
215	<span class="kw">static</span> <span class="kw">constexpr</span> <span class="kw">double</span> NaN = std::<span class="fn">numeric_limits</span>&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt;::<span class="fn">quiet_NaN</span>();
216	
217	<span class="cmt">// Used to fill undefined output positions:</span>
218	<span class="cmt">// — non-positive production in days_of_supply()</span>
219	<span class="cmt">// — first element of fill_rate() (no prior period exists)</span>
220	std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; out(n, NaN); <span class="cmt">// initialize entire output to NaN</span></pre>
221	
222	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
223	  <p class="usage-sentence">"When production_kbd[i] &lt;= 0.0, days_of_supply() writes quiet_NaN into the output vector so downstream Python can detect the gap with math.isnan()."</p>
224	
225	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
226	  <div class="contrast-box">
227	    <strong>vs. signaling_NaN():</strong> <code>std::numeric_limits&lt;double&gt;::signaling_NaN()</code> triggers a hardware floating-point exception on first use (if FP exceptions are enabled) — useful for debugging uninitialized reads, not for sentinel values in production pipelines.<br><br>
228	    <strong>vs. Python float('nan'):</strong> Python's <code>float('nan')</code> is a quiet NaN equivalent at the language level. <code>math.isnan()</code> detects both the C++-produced and Python-produced NaN correctly — they round-trip cleanly through the pybind11 type caster.
229	  </div>
230	</div>
231	
232	<!-- ═══════════════════════ pybind11/stl.h ═══════════════════════ -->
233	<div class="term-card" id="pybind11-stl">
234	  <div class="term-header">
235	    <span class="term-name">pybind11/stl.h</span>
236	    <span class="term-type type-macro">C++ Header</span>
237	  </div>
238	
239	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
240	  <p class="definition">A pybind11 header that registers type casters for C++ Standard Template Library containers (<code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::list</code>, <code>std::map</code>, etc.), enabling automatic copy-conversion to/from Python equivalents. Without it, passing a Python list where a C++ function expects <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> produces a <code>TypeError</code> at the call boundary — pybind11 will not attempt the conversion.</p>
241	
242	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
243	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/bindings.cpp</span>
244	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;pybind11/pybind11.h&gt;</span>
245	<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</span>   <span class="cmt">// &lt;-- activates list ↔ vector type casters</span>
246	
247	<span class="cmt">// Now this binding compiles and works at runtime:</span>
248	m.<span class="fn">def</span>(<span class="str">"fill_rate"</span>, &amp;fill_rate, py::<span class="fn">arg</span>(<span class="str">"storage_kb"</span>));
249	<span class="cmt">// Python list[float] → std::vector&lt;double&gt; (in)</span>
250	<span class="cmt">// std::vector&lt;double&gt; → Python list[float] (out)</span></pre>
251	
252	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
253	  <p class="usage-sentence">"Including pybind11/stl.h is what makes <code>cpp.fill_rate([300.0, 350.0, 330.0])</code> work — the Python list is transparently copied into std::vector&lt;double&gt; before the C++ function executes."</p>
254	
255	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
256	  <div class="contrast-box">
257	    <strong>vs. pybind11/numpy.h:</strong> <code>pybind11/numpy.h</code> provides zero-copy array views via the buffer protocol — C++ reads directly from the NumPy memory without allocating. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> always copies, which is correct for immutable inputs of moderate size but wasteful for large mutable buffers.<br><br>
258	    <strong>Header-only activation:</strong> the type caster is not a runtime plugin — including the header causes the compiler to instantiate the template specialization. Missing the include means the caster simply does not exist at link time.
259	  </div>
260	</div>
261	
262	<!-- ═══════════════════════ type caster ═══════════════════════ -->
263	<div class="term-card" id="type-caster">
264	  <div class="term-header">
265	    <span class="term-name">type caster</span>
266	    <span class="term-type type-concept">pybind11 Concept</span>
267	  </div>
268	
269	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
270	  <p class="definition">A compile-time template specialization in pybind11 that converts C++ types to Python objects (and back) at the function call boundary. Each caster is activated by including the appropriate header; pybind11 resolves which caster to use for each argument and return value at compile time, with no runtime reflection.</p>
271	
272	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
273	  <pre><span class="cmt">// The STL type caster (from pybind11/stl.h) handles both directions</span>
274	<span class="cmt">// for every volume-metrics function:</span>
275	<span class="cmt">//</span>
276	<span class="cmt">//   fill_rate(storage_kb: list[float]) -&gt; list[float]</span>
277	<span class="cmt">//             ─────┬─────               ─────┬─────</span>
278	<span class="cmt">//      Python list (in)         Python list (out, copied from std::vector)</span>
279	<span class="cmt">//</span>
280	<span class="cmt">// Memory timeline per call:</span>
281	<span class="cmt">//   1. Copy: Python list → std::vector&lt;double&gt; (heap alloc)</span>
282	<span class="cmt">//   2. C++ executes fill_rate()</span>
283	<span class="cmt">//   3. Copy: std::vector&lt;double&gt; → Python list (heap alloc)</span></pre>
284	
285	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
286	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The type caster runs on every call: Python list → std::vector&lt;double&gt; (in), C++ std::vector&lt;double&gt; → Python list (out), copying memory twice per call."</p>
287	
288	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
289	  <div class="contrast-box">
290	    <strong>vs. buffer caster (pybind11/numpy.h):</strong> a buffer caster shares memory without copying — C++ receives a pointer into the NumPy array's existing allocation. Zero-copy semantics; better for large arrays at the cost of requiring NumPy on the caller side.<br><br>
291	    <strong>Immutable semantics:</strong> because the STL caster copies, C++ modifications to the vector are not visible to the Python caller after the call returns. This is a feature for correctness (no aliasing surprises), and a cost for performance (two allocations per call).
292	  </div>
293	</div>
