295	<!-- ═══════════════════════ parity test ═══════════════════════ -->
296	<div class="term-card" id="parity-test">
297	  <div class="term-header">
298	    <span class="term-name">parity test</span>
299	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Testing Pattern</span>
300	  </div>
301	
302	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
303	  <p class="definition">A test pattern that runs identical inputs through both a C++ implementation and a simpler Python reference implementation, then asserts the outputs match element-by-element within a tolerance (typically 1e-9). NaN positions require special handling — the tolerance check is skipped and replaced with an <code>isnan()</code> check on both sides.</p>
304	
305	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
306	  <pre><span class="cmt"># tests/test_oil_volume.py</span>
307	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_capacity_utilization_parity</span>():
308	    storage = [<span class="num">60_000.0</span>, <span class="num">65_000.0</span>, <span class="num">70_000.0</span>]
309	    capacity = <span class="num">76_000.0</span>
310	    got      = cpp.<span class="fn">capacity_utilization</span>(storage, capacity)
311	    expected = <span class="fn">_py_capacity_utilization</span>(storage, capacity)
312	    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">all</span>(<span class="fn">_close</span>(g, e) <span class="kw">for</span> g, e <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">zip</span>(got, expected))
313	
314	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_close</span>(a, b, tol=<span class="num">1e-9</span>):
315	    <span class="kw">if</span> math.<span class="fn">isnan</span>(a) <span class="kw">and</span> math.<span class="fn">isnan</span>(b): <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">True</span>
316	    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">abs</span>(a - b) &lt; tol</pre>
317	
318	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
319	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The parity test is the correctness gate: it fails as soon as the C++ implementation diverges from the Python reference, making regressions visible immediately."</p>
320	
321	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
322	  <div class="contrast-box">
323	    <strong>vs. unit test:</strong> a unit test checks a specific expected value known in advance. A parity test is agnostic to the exact value — it only cares that C++ and Python agree. This means a shared bug in both implementations passes a parity test but fails a value-pinned unit test.<br><br>
324	    <strong>NaN handling:</strong> <code>assert nan == nan</code> is always False in IEEE 754. The <code>_close()</code> helper must short-circuit on <code>math.isnan(a) and math.isnan(b)</code> before the subtraction, or the parity test falsely fails on every NaN position.
325	  </div>
326	</div>
327	
328	<!-- ═══════════════════════ APIBudget ═══════════════════════ -->
329	<div class="term-card" id="api-budget">
330	  <div class="term-header">
331	    <span class="term-name">APIBudget</span>
332	    <span class="term-type type-py">Python Class</span>
333	  </div>
334	
335	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
336	  <p class="definition">An object that tracks real network API calls made during a session. <code>budget.charge(source)</code> is called only after a live HTTP request succeeds — never on cache hits — so repeated calls within a session accumulate cost correctly. The budget records which sources were hit and how many times, enabling test assertions about network usage.</p>
337	
338	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
339	  <pre><span class="cmt"># src/oil_alpha/sources.py</span>
340	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_from_cache_or</span>(cache, budget, source, kind, symbol, start, end, fetch):
341	    cached = cache.<span class="fn">load</span>(kind, symbol, start, end)
342	    <span class="kw">if not</span> cached.<span class="fn">empty</span>:
343	        <span class="kw">return</span> cached          <span class="cmt"># cache hit — budget.charge() never called</span>
344	    df = <span class="fn">fetch</span>()               <span class="cmt"># live HTTP request</span>
345	    budget.<span class="fn">charge</span>(source)     <span class="cmt"># only reached after a real network call</span>
346	    cache.<span class="fn">save</span>(df, kind, symbol, start, end)
347	    <span class="kw">return</span> df</pre>
348	
349	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
350	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The test asserts <code>budget.usage.get('eia') is None or budget.usage.get('eia').used == 0</code> after a cache-hit call, proving the APIBudget correctly records zero cost for the second request."</p>
351	
352	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
353	  <div class="contrast-box">
354	    <strong>vs. rate limiter:</strong> a rate limiter prevents a request from going out if a quota would be exceeded. An APIBudget records what already happened — it is diagnostic, not preventive. They complement each other: the budget tells you what was spent; the limiter enforces the cap.<br><br>
355	    <strong>vs. mock counter in tests:</strong> in unit tests you could monkeypatch the fetch function and count calls. APIBudget is the production-grade version: it lives in the actual call path and survives into integration tests and scripts without test-only scaffolding.
356	  </div>
357	</div>
358	
359	<!-- ═══════════════════════ cache hit ═══════════════════════ -->
360	<div class="term-card" id="cache-hit">
361	  <div class="term-header">
362	    <span class="term-name">cache hit</span>
363	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Data Pipeline Concept</span>
364	  </div>
365	
366	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
367	  <p class="definition">A successful retrieval from a local on-disk cache, bypassing a network request entirely. In this repo, <code>cache.load()</code> returns a non-empty DataFrame when the requested data already exists in <code>data_cache/</code> — the outer function returns immediately, charging zero budget and making zero HTTP calls.</p>
368	
369	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
370	  <pre><span class="cmt"># _from_cache_or() in sources.py — the fast path</span>
371	cached = cache.<span class="fn">load</span>(kind, symbol, start, end)
372	<span class="kw">if not</span> cached.<span class="fn">empty</span>:
373	    <span class="kw">return</span> cached   <span class="cmt"># cache hit: no HTTP, no budget.charge(), no cache.save()</span>
374	                    <span class="cmt"># only cost is a file read from data_cache/</span></pre>
375	
376	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
377	  <p class="usage-sentence">"A cache hit in fetch_eia() means: no HTTP call, no budget charge, same DataFrame — the only cost is a file read from data_cache/."</p>
378	
379	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
380	  <div class="contrast-box">
381	    <strong>vs. cache miss:</strong> a miss triggers the full slow path — HTTP call to api.eia.gov, JSON parsing, <code>budget.charge(source)</code>, <code>cache.save()</code> to disk — so the next call for the same range becomes a hit.<br><br>
382	    <strong>Cache key:</strong> the cache is keyed on (kind, symbol, start, end). A request for a wider date range will miss even if a narrower range is cached — the cache does not merge partial hits.
383	  </div>
384	</div>
