386	<!-- ═══════════════════════ EIA v2 API ═══════════════════════ -->
387	<div class="term-card" id="eia-v2-api">
388	  <div class="term-header">
389	    <span class="term-name">EIA v2 API</span>
390	    <span class="term-type type-tool">External API</span>
391	  </div>
392	
393	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
394	  <p class="definition">The U.S. Energy Information Administration's second-generation data API (<code>api.eia.gov/v2/</code>), providing energy statistics via paginated JSON responses with series IDs and API key authentication. Each response contains a nested <code>response.data</code> array of row dicts with <code>period</code> (date string) and <code>value</code> (observation) fields.</p>
395	
396	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
397	  <pre><span class="cmt"># src/oil_alpha/fetch_eia.py — simplified request pattern</span>
398	url = (
399	    <span class="str">f"https://api.eia.gov/v2/seriesid/{series_id}"</span>
400	    <span class="str">f"?api_key={api_key}&amp;offset={offset}&amp;length=5000"</span>
401	)
402	<span class="cmt"># Series used in this repo:</span>
403	<span class="cmt">#   PET.WCSSTUS1.W  — Cushing, OK crude storage (weekly, kb)</span>
404	<span class="cmt">#   PET.MCRFPUS2.M  — US field production (monthly, kbd)</span>
405	rows = resp_json[<span class="str">"response"</span>][<span class="str">"data"</span>]   <span class="cmt"># list of {period, value, ...}</span>
406	df[<span class="str">"date"</span>]  = pd.<span class="fn">to_datetime</span>(df[<span class="str">"period"</span>])
407	df[<span class="str">"value"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[<span class="str">"value"</span>], errors=<span class="str">"coerce"</span>)</pre>
408	
409	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
410	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The EIA v2 API uses <code>period</code> as the date field and <code>value</code> as the observation field in each row dict — the fetcher must rename and coerce these explicitly before returning a tidy DataFrame."</p>
411	
412	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
413	  <div class="contrast-box">
414	    <strong>vs. FRED API:</strong> FRED (fred.stlouisfed.org) serves CSV directly with no API key required for its keyless endpoint. EIA v2 requires a free registered key and returns nested JSON. FRED uses <code>date</code>/<code>value</code> field names already; EIA uses <code>period</code>/<code>value</code> requiring a rename.<br><br>
415	    <strong>Pagination:</strong> EIA v2 caps results at 5,000 rows per request. Long date ranges require looping with incrementing <code>offset</code> parameters until fewer than 5,000 rows are returned.
416	  </div>
417	</div>
418	
419	<!-- ═══════════════════════ capacity utilization ═══════════════════════ -->
420	<div class="term-card" id="capacity-utilization">
421	  <div class="term-header">
422	    <span class="term-name">capacity utilization</span>
423	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Domain Metric</span>
424	  </div>
425	
426	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
427	  <p class="definition">Storage utilization expressed as a percentage of maximum operational capacity: <code>storage_kb / capacity_kb * 100</code>. Values above ~80% signal potential price pressure as storage fills and operators bid down near-month crude to avoid storage congestion. The denominator is fixed at the known operational ceiling, not a physical maximum.</p>
428	
429	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
430	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</span>
431	std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">capacity_utilization</span>(
432	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb,
433	    <span class="kw">double</span> capacity_kb)
434	{
435	    std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; out(storage_kb.<span class="fn">size</span>());
436	    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">size_t</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; storage_kb.<span class="fn">size</span>(); ++i)
437	        out[i] = storage_kb[i] / capacity_kb * <span class="num">100.0</span>;
438	    <span class="kw">return</span> out;
439	}
440	<span class="cmt">// capacity_kb = 76,000 kb (76 million barrels, Cushing DOE figure)</span></pre>
441	
442	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
443	  <p class="usage-sentence">"When capacity_utilization exceeds 85%, crude oil markets typically enter contango as producers discount near-month delivery to avoid storage congestion."</p>
444	
445	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
446	  <div class="contrast-box">
447	    <strong>vs. days of supply:</strong> days of supply is dynamic — it measures how long current storage lasts at the current production rate (storage ÷ throughput). Capacity utilization is static — it measures how full the tank is as a fraction of its ceiling. Both are computed from the same storage input but answer different questions.<br><br>
448	    <strong>Capacity ceiling:</strong> the 76,000 kb figure is the DOE's published operational capacity for Cushing, OK — not the physical shell capacity. The distinction matters: shell capacity is higher, but tanks cannot be filled beyond operational limits without pipeline and pump constraints.
449	  </div>
450	</div>
451	
452	<!-- ═══════════════════════ days of supply ═══════════════════════ -->
453	<div class="term-card" id="days-of-supply">
454	  <div class="term-header">
455	    <span class="term-name">days of supply</span>
456	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Domain Metric</span>
457	  </div>
458	
459	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
460	  <p class="definition">The number of days current storage could supply demand at the current production rate: <code>storage_kb / production_kbd</code>. The units cancel correctly — thousands of barrels divided by thousands of barrels per day yields days. Returns <code>quiet_NaN</code> when production is non-positive to prevent division by zero and silent sentinel contamination.</p>
461	
462	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
463	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</span>
464	std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">days_of_supply</span>(
465	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb,
466	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt;&amp; production_kbd)
467	{
468	    std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; out(n, NaN);
469	    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">size_t</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt; n; ++i)
470	        <span class="kw">if</span> (production_kbd[i] &gt; <span class="num">0.0</span>)
471	            out[i] = storage_kb[i] / production_kbd[i];
472	    <span class="kw">return</span> out;   <span class="cmt">// non-positive positions remain NaN</span>
473	}</pre>
474	
475	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
476	  <p class="usage-sentence">"A days-of-supply reading below 20 days signals tight supply and typically supports higher near-month crude prices."</p>
477	
478	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
479	  <div class="contrast-box">
480	    <strong>vs. capacity utilization:</strong> capacity utilization is a static snapshot (how full is the tank as a fraction of its ceiling?); days of supply is dynamic (how long does the current inventory last at current throughput?). Both use the same storage input but tell different market stories.<br><br>
481	    <strong>Unit note:</strong> storage is in kb (thousands of barrels) and production is in kbd (thousands of barrels per day), so the ratio is already in days without any unit conversion factor.
482	  </div>
483	</div>
484	
485	<!-- ═══════════════════════ fill rate ═══════════════════════ -->
486	<div class="term-card" id="fill-rate">
487	  <div class="term-header">
488	    <span class="term-name">fill rate</span>
489	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Domain Metric</span>
490	  </div>
491	
492	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
493	  <p class="definition">The week-over-week change in storage: <code>storage[i] - storage[i-1]</code>. Positive = injection (build); negative = withdrawal (draw). The first element is always <code>quiet_NaN</code> because there is no prior period. This is a first-difference operator applied to the storage time series — no smoothing, no window.</p>
494	
495	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
496	  <pre><span class="cmt">// cpp/oil_volume/volume_metrics.cpp</span>
497	std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; <span class="fn">fill_rate</span>(
498	    <span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt;&amp; storage_kb)
499	{
500	    std::vector&lt;<span class="kw">double</span>&gt; out(n, NaN); <span class="cmt">// index 0 stays NaN — no prior period</span>
501	    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">size_t</span> i = <span class="num">1</span>; i &lt; n; ++i)
502	        out[i] = storage_kb[i] - storage_kb[i - <span class="num">1</span>];
503	    <span class="kw">return</span> out;
504	}
505	<span class="cmt">// Initializing with NaN means any unfilled slot is automatically correct</span></pre>
506	
507	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
508	  <p class="usage-sentence">"Analysts watch weekly fill_rate to detect whether EIA storage reports will beat or miss consensus estimates, which moves crude prices on release day."</p>
509	
510	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
511	  <div class="contrast-box">
512	    <strong>vs. rolling mean:</strong> rolling mean smooths across a window of N periods; fill rate is a point-to-point first difference with no smoothing. Both operate on the storage series, but fill rate is noisier and more responsive to single-week anomalies.<br><br>
513	    <strong>Sign convention:</strong> positive fill rate = injection (storage increasing, bearish for crude price); negative fill rate = draw (storage decreasing, bullish for crude price). The sign is natural from subtraction — no convention adjustment needed.
514	  </div>
515	</div>
516	
517	<!-- ═══════════════════════ non-positive filter ═══════════════════════ -->
518	<div class="term-card" id="non-positive-filter">
519	  <div class="term-header">
520	    <span class="term-name">non-positive filter</span>
521	    <span class="term-type type-concept">Data Quality Pattern</span>
522	  </div>
523	
524	  <div class="section-label">Definition</div>
525	  <p class="definition">A guard that discards or maps to <code>NaN</code> any value &le; 0, applied at two layers: at ingest time in Python (DataFrame row removal) and at compute time in C++ (conditional NaN assignment). The two-layer approach is defensive — the ingest filter catches bad raw data, while the C++ guard protects against values that slip through or are constructed incorrectly in tests.</p>
526	
527	  <div class="section-label">Project example</div>
528	  <pre><span class="cmt"># Layer 1 — Python ingest (src/oil_alpha/fetch_eia.py)</span>
529	df = df[df[<span class="str">"value"</span>] &gt; <span class="num">0</span>].<span class="fn">copy</span>()   <span class="cmt"># drop zero/negative EIA observations</span>
530	
531	<span class="cmt">// Layer 2 — C++ compute (volume_metrics.cpp, days_of_supply)</span>
532	<span class="kw">if</span> (production_kbd[i] &gt; <span class="num">0.0</span>)
533	    out[i] = storage_kb[i] / production_kbd[i];
534	<span class="cmt">// else: out[i] remains NaN (initialized above)</span></pre>
535	
536	  <div class="section-label">Usage sentence</div>
537	  <p class="usage-sentence">"The non-positive filter runs at two layers: at ingest (fetch_eia cleans the raw data) and at compute time (C++ guards against bad production figures that slipped through)."</p>
538	
539	  <div class="section-label">Contrast</div>
540	  <div class="contrast-box">
541	    <strong>vs. NaN filter:</strong> a NaN filter drops rows where the value is missing (<code>math.isnan</code> or <code>pd.isna</code>). A non-positive filter drops rows where the value is zero or negative — a structurally valid number that is semantically invalid as a production rate. The two filters are complementary: run NaN filter first (remove undecidable), then non-positive filter (remove invalid positives).<br><br>
542	    <strong>Why both layers:</strong> the Python ingest filter runs once at fetch time and results are cached. The C++ guard runs on every call, defending against test inputs, synthetic data, and future code paths that bypass the ingest filter. Defense in depth costs one branch per element — negligible overhead.
543	  </div>
